<td id="S5.T1.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T1.1.5.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">69.4</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-B2 Runtime Analysis ‣ V-B Main Results ‣ V Experiments ‣ ReCIT: Reconstructing Full Private Data from Gradient in Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> highlights the runtime efficiency of ReCIT compared to baseline methods (DLG, LAMP, Grab and DAGER) on the SQuAD v2 dataset using LoRA with Bloomz-3B. ReCIT consistently achieves significantly lower runtimes across all batch sizes. In contrast, DAGER takes about 10 times longer at b = 128. This efficiency is due to ReCIT’s targeted approach, which focuses on recovering fragments of the prefix rather than performing exhaustive gradient matching or full vocabulary checks. The results demonstrate that ReCIT is not only effective in recovering PII but also highly scalable and computationally efficient.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
